      "lineAnnotations": {
        "0": {"synopsis":"Reset: the anti-brick core's boot ROM starts here. Turns interrupts off, selects interrupt\nmode 1 and goes to the start-up code; interrupts stay off from then on"},
        "8": {"synopsis":"RST $08: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "11": {"synopsis":"The compiler's name, in ASCII: \"Z88DK\""},
        "16": {"synopsis":"RST $10: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "19": {"synopsis":"The compiler's version, in ASCII and ended by a zero byte: \"2.30\""},
        "24": {"synopsis":"RST $18: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "27": {"synopsis":"Takes 3 from DE. Not called in this ROM\nEntry: DE = the value.\nExit: DE = the value - 3.\nChanges: DE."},
        "32": {"synopsis":"RST $20: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "35": {"synopsis":"Takes 3 from BC. Not called in this ROM\nEntry: BC = the value.\nExit: BC = the value - 3.\nChanges: BC."},
        "40": {"synopsis":"RST $28: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "43": {"synopsis":"Drops three words from the stack and returns. Not reached in this ROM"},
        "46": {"synopsis":"A lone RET: the unused restarts jump here to return to their caller"},
        "48": {"synopsis":"RST $30: not used; returns at once\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "51": {"synopsis":"CALL $0033 calls the code at IY: a text window's plot routine or a stream's output driver,\nwhose first three bytes are a JP\nEntry: IY = the code's address.\nChanges: whatever that code changes."},
        "52": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the address in HL. Not reached in this ROM"},
        "53": {"synopsis":"Jumps to the address in IX. Not reached in this ROM"},
        "56": {"synopsis":"RST $38 and the mode 1 interrupt: re-enables interrupts and returns. Interrupts are never\nenabled in this ROM\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "59": {"synopsis":"Stores A in the 16 bytes from HL. Not called in this ROM; TEXT_INIT enters it at $0053\nEntry: A = the value; HL = the first byte.\nExit: HL = the byte after the last one.\nChanges: HL."},
        "83": {"synopsis":"Stores A in the 4 bytes from HL: the last four steps of FILL_16\nEntry: A = the value; HL = the first byte.\nExit: HL = the byte after the last one.\nChanges: HL."},
        "92": {"synopsis":"Adds 8 to HL. Not called in this ROM\nEntry: HL = the value.\nExit: HL = the value + 8.\nChanges: HL."},
        "102": {"synopsis":"NMI: returns at once (RETN)\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: none."},
        "105": {"synopsis":"The start-up code: sets the stack to the top of memory, copies the 78 start values to\n$5B00-$5B4D, clears the 1162 bytes of variables at $5B4E-$5FD7, runs MAIN, and stops should\nit return","comment":"SP = $0000: the first push goes to $FFFF"},
        "108": {"comment":"From the start values after the texts"},
        "120": {"comment":"The variables to clear"},
        "126": {"comment":"With 0"},
        "134": {"comment":"Keeps HL as it is"},
        "137": {"synopsis":"Halts again should the processor ever leave the HALT"},
        "139": {"synopsis":"Reads the word HL points at\nEntry: HL = the word's address.\nExit: HL = the word; zero flag set when it is 0.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "145": {"synopsis":"Sets the border colour: keeps it in the copy of port $FE at $5B00 and sends the copy out\nEntry: L = the colour (bits 2-0 are used).\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, L."},
        "161": {"synopsis":"Gives the attribute address of the cell holding a pixel\nEntry: L = the pixel's x (0-255); H = its y (0-191).\nExit: HL = the attribute address.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "181": {"synopsis":"Gives the screen address of the byte holding a pixel\nEntry: L = the pixel's x (0-255); H = its y (0-191).\nExit: HL = the screen address; D = y; E = x.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL."},
        "205": {"synopsis":"Moves a screen address one pixel row down, across character rows and thirds\nEntry: HL = a screen address.\nExit: HL = the address of the byte below it.\nChanges: AF, HL.","comment":"The next pixel row: still in the same character row?"},
        "214": {"comment":"The next character row: still in the same third?"},
        "228": {"synopsis":"Turns an upper-case letter into lower case; any other code is kept\nEntry: A = the code.\nExit: A = the code, lower case.\nChanges: AF."},
        "240": {"synopsis":"Marks a failure: stores 255 in the error number at $5B4E and returns 0 with carry set\nEntry: none.\nExit: HL = 0; carry set.\nChanges: F, HL."},
        "251": {"synopsis":"Drops three words from the stack and returns 0 with carry set"},
        "254": {"synopsis":"Returns 0 with carry set"},
        "262": {"synopsis":"Returns 0 with carry clear"},
        "271": {"synopsis":"Returns -1 with carry set"},
        "279": {"synopsis":"Marks a failure: stores 255 in the error number at $5B4E and returns -1 with carry set"},
        "289": {"synopsis":"Prints a text in a text window, both given on the stack, which the routine takes off: the\ncaller pushes the text's address, then the window's, and calls\nEntry: on the stack, the window on top, the text under it.\nExit: HL = the characters printed; HL = minus that when the text ran off the bottom.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF'.\nKeeps: IX."},
        "301": {"synopsis":"Sets up a text window, given on the stack, which the routine takes off: the caller pushes\nthe limits' address, the font's and the window's, and calls\nEntry: on the stack, the window on top, then the font, then the 8 limit bytes.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "309": {"synopsis":"Gives the width of a text in a font, both given on the stack, which the routine takes off:\nthe caller pushes the text's address, then the font's, and calls\nEntry: on the stack, the font on top, the text under it.\nExit: HL = the width in pixels.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "316": {"synopsis":"Prints a text, ended by a zero byte, in a text window. A newline (code 10) starts a new\nline; a character that does not fit across starts a new line and is printed there; one that\ndoes not fit down ends the printing\nEntry: DE = the text; IY = the window.\nExit: HL = the characters printed; carry set when the text ran off the bottom.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF'.\nKeeps: IX, IY."},
        "319": {"synopsis":"The next character; the end?"},
        "324": {"comment":"A newline?"},
        "333": {"synopsis":"Draws the character; did it fit?"},
        "339": {"synopsis":"Counts the character and goes on"},
        "345": {"synopsis":"It did not fit: across (A = 0) or down (A = 1)?"},
        "351": {"synopsis":"Not across: a new line, then the same character again"},
        "358": {"synopsis":"Starts a new line in a text window: x back to the left margin, the overflow flag cleared, y\ndown by the font's line height\nEntry: IY = the window.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, DE, HL."},
        "380": {"synopsis":"Gives a text window's y one line further down\nEntry: IY = the window.\nExit: HL = y + the font's line height.\nChanges: F, DE, HL."},
        "397": {"synopsis":"Sets up a text window, the 24-byte block a window's routines find at IY\n1. +0: JP $020D, the routine that puts a row of pixels on the screen (XOR_PLOT).\n2. +3: the font's address.\n3. +5: x, +6: x's overflow flag, +7: y (a word); all 0.\n4. +9: x origin, +$0B: width (a word), +$0D: y origin, +$0F: height (a word): the 8 bytes\n   given.\n5. +$11: the left margin, 3; +$12 to +$15: 0.\nThe caller sets +$16 and +$17 after it: the attribute value and the mask the cells under a\ncharacter get\nEntry: HL = the window; BC = the font; DE = the 8 limit bytes.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "406": {"comment":"The font"},
        "410": {"comment":"x, its flag and y: 0"},
        "415": {"comment":"The limits"},
        "421": {"comment":"The left margin"},
        "427": {"synopsis":"Gives the width of a text in a font: each character's advance + 1 and the font's gap, less\nthe last gap\nEntry: HL = the font; DE = the text.\nExit: HL = the width in pixels; HL = 0 for an empty text.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "428": {"comment":"Empty?"},
        "437": {"synopsis":"Adds the next character's width and the gap"},
        "453": {"comment":"The end?"},
        "461": {"synopsis":"Gives how far a character moves the pen: its width less its left bearing\nEntry: A = the code; HL = the font.\nExit: A = the advance in pixels.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL."},
        "468": {"synopsis":"Looks a character up in a font\nEntry: A = the code; HL = the font.\nExit: HL = the bitmap's address; A = the left bearing (0-3); B = the width; C = the blank\nrows above the bitmap.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL."},
        "474": {"comment":"The offset's top 2 bits: the bearing"},
        "485": {"comment":"The width in bits 3-0, the blank rows in bits 7-4"},
        "501": {"synopsis":"Finds a character's three-byte entry in a font; codes below 32 and past the last one give\n\"?\"\nEntry: A = the code; HL = the font.\nExit: HL = the entry.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL."},
        "507": {"synopsis":"Past the font's last code?"},
        "515": {"synopsis":"The entry: font + 3 + (code - 32) * 3"},
        "525": {"synopsis":"Puts a row of a character on the screen: XORs three bytes into the screen bytes from HL,\nleft to right. A text window's first bytes jump here\nEntry: HL = the leftmost byte; D, C, A = the three bytes, left to right.\nExit: HL as on entry.\nChanges: AF."},
        "538": {"synopsis":"Draws a character at a text window's pen and moves the pen on\n1. The glyph is looked up (\"?\" for codes the font lacks); a space is widened by bits 3-0 of\n   the window's +$13.\n2. Across: the pen goes back by the bearing, never left of the margin; the character must\n   end inside the width, unless the window has no right edge.\n3. Down: the line must end inside the height.\n4. Unless the attribute mask is $FF and the value 0, the cells under it are recoloured:\n   (attribute AND mask) OR value.\n5. Each bitmap row is shifted right to the pen's pixel and XORed onto the screen through\n   the window's plot routine.\n6. The pen moves on by the width and the font's gap; past 255, the overflow flag is set.\nEntry: C = the code; IY = the window.\nExit: carry clear when drawn; carry set when not, with A = 0 when it does not fit across\nand A = 1 when it does not fit down.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF'.\nKeeps: IX, IY."},
        "544": {"comment":"A' = the font's line height"},
        "547": {"comment":"The font's gap"},
        "559": {"synopsis":"Not in the font: \"?\" instead"},
        "561": {"synopsis":"The entry: font + 3 + index * 3"},
        "568": {"comment":"A space?"},
        "571": {"comment":"The window's extra space width"},
        "577": {"synopsis":"Reads the entry: the offset and bearing, the width and blank rows, where the bitmap ends"},
        "610": {"comment":"Pen already past 255?"},
        "620": {"comment":"Not left of the margin"},
        "627": {"synopsis":"Takes the bearing off the pen","comment":"Less the bearing, not below 0"},
        "631": {"synopsis":"Keeps the pen's new x; does the character fit across?"},
        "635": {"comment":"Past 255?"},
        "639": {"comment":"Inside the width?"},
        "644": {"comment":"No right edge?"},
        "651": {"synopsis":"Fits across: works out the pixel x and checks down","comment":"The pixel x: the pen + the x origin"},
        "656": {"comment":"y past 255?"},
        "668": {"comment":"Past 255 at the bottom?"},
        "673": {"comment":"Inside the height?"},
        "678": {"comment":"No bottom edge?"},
        "685": {"synopsis":"Fits down: the pixel y and the rows to draw"},
        "689": {"comment":"The pixel y: the pen + the blank rows + the y origin"},
        "695": {"comment":"Colouring wanted?"},
        "738": {"synopsis":"Recolours one row of cells"},
        "743": {"synopsis":"The next cell of the row","comment":"(attribute AND mask) OR value"},
        "761": {"synopsis":"Draws the bitmap: one byte per row for a width below 8, else two"},
        "764": {"comment":"The screen address of the first row"},
        "774": {"comment":"Narrower than 8?"},
        "781": {"synopsis":"The bitmap's end?"},
        "785": {"synopsis":"The row's byte or bytes"},
        "796": {"synopsis":"Shifted right to the pixel x?"},
        "801": {"synopsis":"One shift right over the three bytes"},
        "809": {"synopsis":"Puts the row on the screen and moves down a row"},
        "821": {"synopsis":"Moves the pen on: the width, the gap and one"},
        "832": {"comment":"Past 255?"},
        "839": {"synopsis":"Does not fit across: A = 0"},
        "843": {"synopsis":"Does not fit down, with one more word to drop"},
        "844": {"synopsis":"Does not fit down: A = 1"},
        "849": {"synopsis":"Is a key held? A key is given as its keyboard row and its bit in that row, and whether CAPS\nSHIFT and SYMBOL SHIFT must be held with it\nEntry: L = the row's port high byte ($FE, $FD, ... $7F); H: bits 4-0 = the key's bit, bit 7\n= with CAPS SHIFT, bit 6 = with SYMBOL SHIFT.\nExit: HL = -1 (carry set) when held, HL = 0 (carry clear) when not.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "854": {"comment":"With CAPS SHIFT?"},
        "857": {"comment":"CAPS SHIFT: row $FE, bit 0"},
        "866": {"synopsis":"With SYMBOL SHIFT?"},
        "870": {"comment":"SYMBOL SHIFT: row $7F, bit 1"},
        "879": {"synopsis":"The key itself: its bit 0 when held"},
        "891": {"synopsis":"The mode 1 interrupt's handler: enables interrupts and returns"},
        "894": {"synopsis":"The NMI's handler: returns from the NMI"},
        "896": {"synopsis":"Limits a count to 32767: a negative one gives 32767\nEntry: HL = the count.\nExit: HL = the count, at most 32767.\nChanges: F, HL."},
        "903": {"synopsis":"Reads an unsigned decimal number from a text\nEntry: DE = the first digit.\nExit: HL = the number; DE = the first character after it; carry set when it passed 65535.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "908": {"synopsis":"The next character: a digit?"},
        "919": {"comment":"HL * 10 + the digit, each step checked for overflow"},
        "940": {"synopsis":"Past 65535: carry set"},
        "943": {"synopsis":"Negates HL\nEntry: HL = the value.\nExit: HL = -value.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "951": {"synopsis":"Adds DE to HL, giving 65535 when the sum would pass it\nEntry: HL and DE = the values.\nExit: HL = the sum, at most 65535.\nChanges: F, HL."},
        "957": {"synopsis":"Gives the smaller of HL and BC, unsigned\nEntry: HL and BC = the values.\nExit: HL = the smaller.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "965": {"synopsis":"BC is the smaller"},
        "968": {"synopsis":"Adds BC to HL, giving 65535 when the sum would pass it\nEntry: HL and BC = the values.\nExit: HL = the sum, at most 65535.\nChanges: F, HL."},
        "974": {"synopsis":"Turns a digit's value into its character: 0-9, then A-Z\nEntry: A = the value (0-35).\nExit: A = the character.\nChanges: AF."},
        "981": {"synopsis":"10 or more: a letter"},
        "984": {"synopsis":"Is a number base one the conversion supports, 2 to 36?\nEntry: BC = the base.\nExit: carry set when it is.\nChanges: AF."},
        "997": {"synopsis":"32-bit multiplication of two long words on the stack, which the routine takes off: the\ncaller pushes each one high word first, and calls\nEntry: on the stack, one long word on top, the other under it.\nExit: DEHL = the product's low 32 bits.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "998": {"comment":"DEHL' = the long word on top"},
        "1008": {"synopsis":"MUL32 when both high words are 0: a 16 by 16 bit multiplication"},
        "1014": {"synopsis":"32-bit multiplication with the Z80N's MUL, keeping the product's low 32 bits\n1. Both high words 0: MUL16X16 does it.\n2. Otherwise the 8 by 8 bit products that reach the low 32 bits are added up.\nEntry: DEHL = one factor; DEHL' = the other.\nExit: DEHL = the product's low 32 bits; A = 0.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "1020": {"comment":"Both high words 0?"},
        "1124": {"synopsis":"16 by 16 bit multiplication with the Z80N's MUL: four 8 by 8 bit products\nEntry: HL and DE = the factors.\nExit: DEHL = the 32-bit product.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "1157": {"synopsis":"Unsigned division of a 32-bit dividend by a 16-bit divisor, which must not be 0: 32 passes\nof shift and subtract over both register sets. Called with carry clear\nEntry: DEHL = the dividend; BC = the divisor; carry clear.\nExit: DEHL = the quotient; DEHL' = the remainder.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "1159": {"comment":"DEHL' = the divisor"},
        "1163": {"comment":"Carry set: check for 0 first"},
        "1170": {"synopsis":"Dividing by 0?"},
        "1176": {"synopsis":"Sets up the passes: the dividend's low word in BC and its high word in BC' and A, the\nremainder (0) in HL and HL'"},
        "1193": {"synopsis":"One pass: shifts the next dividend bit into the remainder and tries to take the divisor off"},
        "1214": {"comment":"Divisor fitted?"},
        "1221": {"synopsis":"Carry = the quotient bit, shifted in at the next pass"},
        "1242": {"synopsis":"Division by zero: marks the failure and returns -1"},
        "1261": {"synopsis":"Unsigned 16-bit division with a divisor of 256 or more: one quotient bit per pass, 8 passes"},
        "1264": {"comment":"Divisor fits?"},
        "1269": {"synopsis":"The next pass; A gathers the quotient's bits inverted","comment":"Carry = the quotient bit"},
        "1283": {"synopsis":"Unsigned division of HL by an 8-bit divisor: 16 passes, the remainder in A\nEntry: HL = the dividend; E = the divisor, not 0.\nExit: HL = the quotient; DE = A = the remainder; carry clear.\nChanges: AF, B, DE, HL."},
        "1287": {"synopsis":"One quotient bit: shifts the next dividend bit into A"},
        "1289": {"comment":"A went past 8 bits?"},
        "1291": {"comment":"Below the divisor?"},
        "1294": {"synopsis":"Takes the divisor from A and sets the quotient bit"},
        "1296": {"synopsis":"The next pass; then DE = the remainder"},
        "1301": {"synopsis":"Division by zero: marks the failure and returns -1"},
        "1305": {"synopsis":"Beeps, the pitch and length given on the stack, which the routine takes off: the caller\npushes the frequency, then the length, and calls. The timing assumes 3.5 MHz\nEntry: on the stack, the length in ms on top, the frequency in Hz under it.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "1311": {"synopsis":"Beeps through the speaker bit of port $FE\n1. The number of cycles is frequency * length / 1000.\n2. The delay for half a cycle is 437500 / frequency - 30, in the loop's units.\nEntry: HL = the frequency in Hz; DE = the length in ms.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "1315": {"comment":"Frequency * length / 1000: the cycles"},
        "1326": {"comment":"437500: the loop's units per second, halved"},
        "1333": {"comment":"Less the overhead of a cycle"},
        "1342": {"synopsis":"Plays DE cycles with HL as the half-cycle delay\n1. The delay's two low bits pick how many of the NOPs at $0556 each half cycle runs.\n2. Each half cycle waits, then flips the speaker bit and sends the border copy to port $FE.\n3. At the end the copy at $5B00 takes the final speaker bit."},
        "1344": {"comment":"No cycles?"},
        "1351": {"comment":"3 - the delay's two low bits"},
        "1357": {"comment":"IY = the first NOP to run"},
        "1366": {"synopsis":"Up to three NOPs that tune the half cycle's length; reached by JP (IY)"},
        "1371": {"synopsis":"The delay loop: C counts L / 4 and B counts H"},
        "1380": {"comment":"Flips the speaker bit"},
        "1386": {"comment":"Speaker bit now set?"},
        "1399": {"synopsis":"The first half of a cycle is over: waits for the second"},
        "1403": {"synopsis":"The last cycle is over"},
        "1406": {"synopsis":"Keeps the border and speaker bits of a port $FE value in the copy at $5B00\nEntry: A = the value.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, C."},
        "1417": {"synopsis":"Gives the border and speaker bits of the port $FE copy at $5B00\nEntry: none.\nExit: A = the copy's bits 0-2 and 4.\nChanges: AF."},
        "1423": {"synopsis":"Formats a text into an output stream: the stream is checked first. Not called in this ROM:\nSPRINTF enters at FORMAT_OUT_GO\nEntry: IY = the stream; DE = the format; BC = the first argument's address.\nExit: HL = the characters written (at most 32767); carry set and HL negative on failure.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "1427": {"synopsis":"The formatter: copies the format's text to the stream and replaces each conversion (\"%\" with\nflags, width, precision, length and a letter) by its argument, kept in a 44-byte frame on\nthe stack. HL' counts the characters written. Only \"%s\" and \"%u\" are known, and \"%%\" gives\na \"%\"\nEntry: IY = the stream; DE = the format; BC = the first argument's address.\nExit: HL = the characters written (at most 32767); carry set and HL negative on failure.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, AF', BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes.","comment":"The frame"},
        "1433": {"comment":"No characters written yet"},
        "1438": {"synopsis":"Copies the text before the next \"%\""},
        "1440": {"synopsis":"Finds the next \"%\" or the end; writes what comes before it"},
        "1456": {"comment":"The stream failed?"},
        "1459": {"comment":"The end of the format?"},
        "1465": {"comment":"\"%%\"?"},
        "1474": {"synopsis":"The end: drops the frame and returns the count"},
        "1487": {"synopsis":"The format flags FORMAT_FLAGS knows, a character and its bit in the flags byte each: \"+\"\n$40, \" \" $20, \"#\" $10, \"0\" $08, \"-\" $04"},
        "1497": {"synopsis":"Reads a conversion's flags into C, from the table at $05CF, and keeps them at the stream's\n+5"},
        "1500": {"synopsis":"The next character: a flag?"},
        "1507": {"synopsis":"Looks the character up in the table"},
        "1517": {"comment":"\"*\": the width from the arguments?"},
        "1528": {"comment":"A negative width: its size, left-aligned"},
        "1539": {"synopsis":"Keeps the width"},
        "1544": {"synopsis":"A flag: adds its bit"},
        "1549": {"synopsis":"The width as digits"},
        "1552": {"comment":"Past 65535 or 32767: a failure"},
        "1562": {"synopsis":"The precision: 1 unless a \".\" gives one"},
        "1566": {"comment":"\".\"?"},
        "1576": {"comment":"\"*\": the precision from the arguments?"},
        "1598": {"synopsis":"Keeps the precision"},
        "1603": {"synopsis":"The precision as digits"},
        "1614": {"synopsis":"Keeps the precision read"},
        "1616": {"synopsis":"Reads the length modifier and the conversion's letter, and finds its handler: \"%u\" and \"%s\"\nwithout a length modifier, only \"%s\" with one"},
        "1637": {"synopsis":"Looks in the \"%s\" table"},
        "1645": {"comment":"Unknown: a failure"},
        "1654": {"synopsis":"With a length modifier: only \"%s\""},
        "1658": {"synopsis":"Found: sets the flags the conversion's entry gives and calls its handler, which comes back\nto FORMAT_NEXT"},
        "1661": {"comment":"A \"0\" prefix, not \"0x\"?"},
        "1669": {"synopsis":"Bit 4 clears the \"#\" flag"},
        "1677": {"synopsis":"Keeps the entry's bits 7-6 at the stream's +4 and fills in the frame for the handler"},
        "1714": {"synopsis":"Looks a character up in a conversion table: a character, a flags byte and an address per\nentry, ended by a zero byte\nEntry: HL = the table; B = the character.\nExit: carry set when found, with A = the flags and HL = the handler; carry clear when not.\nChanges: AF, B, HL."},
        "1726": {"synopsis":"Found"},
        "1734": {"synopsis":"The conversions for \"%s\" and \"%u\": two tables, each a character, a flags byte and the\nhandler's address, ended by a zero byte. $06C6: \"s\" with $80 to $07FA (FMT_STRING); $06CB:\n\"u\" with $90 to $0842 (FMT_UNSIGNED)"},
        "1750": {"synopsis":"Back from a handler: the next argument, then the rest of the format"},
        "1753": {"comment":"The handler failed?"},
        "1764": {"synopsis":"A width or precision that could not be read"},
        "1765": {"synopsis":"A failure: marks it"},
        "1768": {"synopsis":"Drops the frame and returns minus the count, minus 1"},
        "1771": {"synopsis":"Drops the frame"},
        "1785": {"synopsis":"A handler failed"},
        "1790": {"synopsis":"Formats a text into memory, the arguments on the stack, as the formatter does for a stream;\nthe result is ended by a zero byte. The caller pushes the arguments, last first, then the\nformat's address and the buffer's, calls, and takes them off after\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, the buffer, then the format, then the\narguments.\nExit: HL = the characters written.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF', BC', DE', HL'."},
        "1793": {"comment":"HL' = the buffer"},
        "1804": {"synopsis":"Makes a stream on the stack whose driver writes to memory (JP MEM_DRIVER, flags $80), runs\nthe formatter, and ends the text with a zero byte"},
        "1834": {"synopsis":"The driver of a stream that writes to memory: DE' is where the next character goes\n1. Operation 1: one character, E', HL times.\n2. Any other: BC bytes copied from HL.","comment":"Operation 1?"},
        "1848": {"synopsis":"One character: just once?"},
        "1860": {"synopsis":"The character several times"},
        "1870": {"synopsis":"Reads a conversion's length modifier from the table at $0770; \"ll\" and \"hh\" give the next\nbit up\nEntry: DE = the character after the precision.\nExit: C = the modifier's bit, 0 for none; DE = the character after it.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "1876": {"synopsis":"Is it this one?"},
        "1886": {"synopsis":"Found: is it \"l\" or \"h\" doubled?"},
        "1895": {"synopsis":"Doubled?"},
        "1904": {"synopsis":"The length modifiers FORMAT_LENGTH knows, a character and its bit each: \"l\" $10, \"L\" $01,\n\"h\" $02, \"j\" $08, \"t\" $40, \"z\" $80"},
        "1916": {"synopsis":"Reads a word and moves past it\nEntry: HL = the word's address.\nExit: DE = the word; HL = the address after it.\nChanges: DE, HL."},
        "1921": {"synopsis":"Reads the first argument a caller pushed: the word under this routine's caller's return\naddress\nEntry: none.\nExit: DE = the argument; HL = the address after it.\nChanges: DE, HL."},
        "1928": {"synopsis":"Checks that a stream can be written to: bits 7 and 3 of its +3 must be 1 and 0. A pending\noperation (bit 1 of +4) is finished first\nEntry: IY = the stream.\nExit: carry clear when it can; carry set and HL = -1 when not.\nChanges: AF, HL; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "1957": {"synopsis":"Cannot be written to: already failed (bit 3)?"},
        "1972": {"synopsis":"Finishes what a stream has pending through its driver: operation 6, then operation 8\nEntry: IY = the stream.\nExit: carry clear and HL = 0 when done; carry set and HL = -1 when not.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "2014": {"synopsis":"Operation 8"},
        "2025": {"synopsis":"Finishes every stream in the list at $5B50. The list is empty in this ROM"},
        "2028": {"synopsis":"The next stream; the end of the list?"},
        "2042": {"synopsis":"\"%s\": writes a text, cut to the precision when one is given, padded with spaces to the\nwidth, on the left unless \"-\" is given; a null pointer writes \"<null>\""},
        "2046": {"comment":"A null pointer?"},
        "2052": {"synopsis":"The length, at most the precision"},
        "2061": {"synopsis":"The padding: the width less the length"},
        "2073": {"synopsis":"Right-aligned: the padding first?"},
        "2090": {"synopsis":"Left-aligned: the text, then the padding"},
        "2102": {"synopsis":"\"<null>\": what \"%s\" prints for a null pointer"},
        "2109": {"synopsis":"Reads the word HL points at\nEntry: HL = the word's address.\nExit: HL = the word; A = its low byte.\nChanges: A, HL."},
        "2114": {"synopsis":"\"%u\": writes an unsigned number in decimal, with the precision as its least number of digits\nand padded to the width"},
        "2140": {"synopsis":"Reads the argument; 0?"},
        "2147": {"synopsis":"Turns the number into digits in the frame, in the base in C"},
        "2160": {"synopsis":"The number is 0: no digits unless the precision asks for them"},
        "2173": {"synopsis":"Works out the zeros the precision needs, then the room the sign and a \"0x\" take, then the\nspaces the width needs"},
        "2193": {"synopsis":"A sign?"},
        "2201": {"synopsis":"\"#\": room for \"0x\"?"},
        "2215": {"synopsis":"The spaces: the width less what is written"},
        "2223": {"synopsis":"No spaces needed"},
        "2224": {"synopsis":"\"0\" without a precision: the spaces become zeros"},
        "2240": {"synopsis":"Left-aligned?"},
        "2255": {"synopsis":"Writes the sign, the \"0x\", the zeros and the digits\nEntry: IY = the stream; HL = the digits' end; BC = their count; DE = the zeros.\nExit: carry set when the stream failed.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "2298": {"synopsis":"The prefix done"},
        "2299": {"synopsis":"Writes the sign and prefix"},
        "2317": {"synopsis":"The digits, lower case unless upper case was asked for"},
        "2338": {"synopsis":"No sign or prefix: the zeros"},
        "2342": {"synopsis":"Left-aligned: the number, then the spaces"},
        "2352": {"synopsis":"Puts the sign the flags ask for: \"-\", \"+\" or \" \"\nEntry: IY = the stream; HL = where it goes.\nExit: HL = the place after it.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "2362": {"synopsis":"\"+\"?"},
        "2369": {"synopsis":"\" \"?"},
        "2375": {"synopsis":"BC = HL, then PUT_ZEROS"},
        "2377": {"synopsis":"Writes BC zeros to the stream\nEntry: IY = the stream; BC = the count.\nExit: carry set when the stream failed.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "2381": {"synopsis":"BC = HL, then PUT_SPACES\nEntry: IY = the stream; HL = the count.\nExit: carry set when the stream failed.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "2383": {"synopsis":"Writes BC spaces to the stream"},
        "2385": {"synopsis":"Writes the character in E BC times: driver operation 1"},
        "2392": {"synopsis":"Writes BC bytes from HL to the stream: driver operation 2\nEntry: IY = the stream; HL = the bytes; BC = the count.\nExit: carry set when the stream failed.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'; whatever the stream's driver changes."},
        "2397": {"synopsis":"Calls the stream's driver and adds BC to the count in HL', which stops at 65535"},
        "2407": {"comment":"The driver failed?"},
        "2413": {"synopsis":"Returns, the count back in HL'"},
        "2418": {"synopsis":"The driver failed: bit 3 of the stream's +3"},
        "2439": {"synopsis":"Turns a number into digits: they are pushed on the stack, last first, then stored from the\nbuffer in the order they are read\nEntry: HL = the number; DE = the buffer; BC = the base (2-36).\nExit: HL = the last digit's address.\nChanges: AF, B, DE, HL, IY."},
        "2446": {"synopsis":"The next digit: the remainder of a division by the base"},
        "2464": {"synopsis":"Stores the digits"},
        "2471": {"synopsis":"Compares two blocks given on the stack, which the routine takes off: the caller pushes the\nlength, the second block's address and the first block's address, and calls\nEntry: on the stack, the first block on top, then the second, then the length.\nExit: HL = 0 when the blocks are the same, else nonzero (H = the difference of the bytes).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2479": {"synopsis":"Compares two blocks byte by byte\nEntry: DE = the first block; HL = the second; BC = the length.\nExit: HL = 0 when the blocks are the same, else nonzero (H = the difference of the bytes).\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2483": {"synopsis":"Compares the next two bytes"},
        "2486": {"comment":"Bytes differ?"},
        "2492": {"synopsis":"The blocks are the same: HL = BC, which is 0 here"},
        "2495": {"synopsis":"The blocks differ: H = the first byte - the second, nonzero"},
        "2499": {"synopsis":"Copies a block\nEntry: HL = the source; DE = the destination; BC = the length.\nExit: HL = the destination; carry clear.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2509": {"synopsis":"Nothing to copy: returns the destination"},
        "2512": {"synopsis":"Looks for a block of bytes inside another\nEntry: IY = the block searched; HL = its length; DE = the bytes looked for; BC = their\ncount.\nExit: HL = the first place they are found, carry clear; HL = 0 and carry set when they are\nnot.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL, IY."},
        "2531": {"synopsis":"Finds the next place the first byte is"},
        "2547": {"synopsis":"Compares the rest"},
        "2556": {"synopsis":"Found: HL = its first byte"},
        "2561": {"synopsis":"Not here: any place left?"},
        "2571": {"synopsis":"Fills a block with one value\nEntry: HL = the block; BC = the length; E = the value.\nExit: HL = the block.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "2588": {"synopsis":"Finds a character in a text ended by a zero byte\nEntry: HL = the text; C = the character.\nExit: HL = the character, zero flag set; at the end, HL = the zero byte and carry set.\nChanges: AF, HL."},
        "2598": {"synopsis":"Compares two texts ended by zero bytes\nEntry: DE = the first text; HL = the second.\nExit: HL = 0 and zero flag set when they are the same; else H = the difference of the bytes.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2611": {"synopsis":"They differ"},
        "2615": {"synopsis":"Gives the length of a text, counting at most BC characters\nEntry: HL = the text; BC = the most to count.\nExit: HL = the length.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2628": {"synopsis":"The length: where it stopped less the start"},
        "2631": {"synopsis":"Turns the letters of a block into lower case\nEntry: HL = the block; BC = its length.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "2635": {"synopsis":"The next byte"},
        "2647": {"synopsis":"Waits HL milliseconds: DELAY_T with 3457 T-states for each millisecond but the last, and\n3446 for the last, which also pays for this routine's own overhead The counts assume 3.5\nMHz: MAIN also calls it at 28 MHz, where it waits an eighth as long\nEntry: HL = the number of milliseconds, at least 1.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2649": {"synopsis":"One millisecond"},
        "2662": {"synopsis":"The last millisecond"},
        "2668": {"synopsis":"Waits HL T-states, the call included, at 3.5 MHz timing\n1. 141 are taken off for the fixed part.\n2. The loop takes 23 per pass while that many remain.\n3. The branches after it make up the rest.\nEntry: HL = the T-states, at least 141.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL."},
        "2675": {"synopsis":"One pass of 23 T-states"},
        "2689": {"synopsis":"An INC HL that only spends time, on the paths that need it"},
        "2690": {"synopsis":"Spends the remainder's low three bits: a set bit 0, 1 or 2 makes its branch 1, 2 or 4\nT-states longer"},
        "2691": {"comment":"Bit 0 set?"},
        "2694": {"synopsis":"The remainder's bit 1"},
        "2695": {"comment":"Bit 1 clear?"},
        "2699": {"synopsis":"The remainder's bit 2: RET NC returns sooner when it is clear"},
        "2702": {"synopsis":"Gives the cluster that follows one in the file allocation table. The table's sector is read\ninto the buffer at $5B7B unless it is the one read last (kept at $5D7C)\n1. FAT32 ($5B54 nonzero): 128 entries of 4 bytes per sector; FAT16: 256 entries of 2 bytes.\n2. The sector wanted ($5D84) is the table's start ($5B55) plus the cluster / entries.\n3. The entry's offset within the sector goes to $5D80.\n4. A FAT32 entry's top 4 bits are dropped.\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, the cluster as a long word (low word on top).\nExit: DEHL = the next cluster; DEHL = 0 when the sector cannot be read.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "2710": {"comment":"FAT32?"},
        "2734": {"comment":"The cluster / 128: seven shifts right"},
        "2736": {"synopsis":"One shift right of the four bytes at $5D84"},
        "2752": {"comment":"The entry within the sector: the cluster's low 7 bits"},
        "2770": {"synopsis":"FAT16: the sector is the cluster's top three bytes, the entry its low byte"},
        "2804": {"synopsis":"The sector already in the buffer?"},
        "2832": {"synopsis":"Reads the sector: the table's start + the sector wanted"},
        "2868": {"comment":"Read?"},
        "2878": {"synopsis":"Read: remembers the sector at $5D7C"},
        "2899": {"synopsis":"Picks the entry out of the buffer"},
        "2907": {"comment":"FAT32?"},
        "2929": {"comment":"The top 4 bits dropped"},
        "2934": {"synopsis":"A FAT16 entry: 2 bytes, the high word 0"},
        "2946": {"synopsis":"Keeps the entry at $5D80-$5D83 and returns it in DEHL"},
        "2960": {"synopsis":"Returns"},
        "2963": {"synopsis":"Mounts the FAT16 or FAT32 file system on the SD card: reads its boot sector and works out\nwhere the tables, the root directory and the data are\n1. The cached table sector is forgotten; FAT16 is assumed ($5B54 = 0).\n2. Sector 0 is read. With neither file-system name in it, it is taken as a partition table:\n   the first partition's start goes to $5D88, the table's last two bytes must be $55 and $AA\n   (either order), and the partition's first sector is read.\n3. That sector must name FAT32 (then $5B54 = 1) or FAT16, end with $55 $AA, start with a\n   jump ($E9 or $EB), and have 512 bytes per sector.\n4. The geometry goes to the variables at $5B55-$5B7A (FAT_MOUNT_GEOM, FAT_MOUNT_FAT32,\n   FAT_MOUNT_FAT16).\nEntry: none.\nExit: L = 1 when mounted, L = 0 when not.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "2976": {"comment":"No table sector cached yet"},
        "2985": {"comment":"FAT16 until FAT32 is found"},
        "2998": {"comment":"Sector 0 into the buffer"},
        "3003": {"comment":"Read?"},
        "3009": {"synopsis":"Sector 0 is in the buffer: the partition starts at 0 unless sector 0 is a partition table\n($5D7B = 1 until a file-system name is found)"},
        "3023": {"comment":"Is \"FAT16   \" at offset $36?"},
        "3046": {"synopsis":"Is \"FAT32   \" at offset $52?"},
        "3069": {"synopsis":"A partition table?"},
        "3089": {"comment":"The first partition's start sector, at offset $1C6"},
        "3109": {"comment":"The table's last two bytes"},
        "3117": {"comment":"$AA then $55?"},
        "3140": {"synopsis":"$55 then $AA?"},
        "3150": {"synopsis":"Not mounted"},
        "3155": {"synopsis":"Reads the partition's first sector into the buffer"},
        "3172": {"comment":"Read?"},
        "3178": {"synopsis":"Is \"FAT32   \" at offset $52?"},
        "3204": {"synopsis":"Is \"FAT16   \" at offset $36?"},
        "3228": {"synopsis":"Does the sector end with $55 $AA?"},
        "3242": {"synopsis":"No: not mounted"},
        "3247": {"synopsis":"Does the sector start with a jump?"},
        "3263": {"synopsis":"512 bytes per sector (offset $0B)?"},
        "3276": {"synopsis":"No: not mounted"},
        "3281": {"synopsis":"The geometry both file systems share\n1. $5B6B = the sectors per cluster (offset $0D); $5B6F = that - 1, a mask.\n2. $5B55 = the partition's start + the reserved sectors (offset $0E): the first table.\n3. $5B69 = the number of tables (offset $10)."},
        "3389": {"comment":"FAT32?"},
        "3396": {"synopsis":"FAT32: the root directory is a cluster chain\n1. $5B73 = the sectors per cluster * 16: the directory entries in a cluster.\n2. $5B65 = the sectors per cluster.\n3. $5B77 = the sectors per table (offset $24).\n4. $5B59 = the first table + the tables * the sectors per table: the first data sector.\n5. $5B5D = the root directory's first cluster (offset $2C), its top 4 bits dropped.\n6. $5B61 = the first data sector + (that cluster - 2) * the sectors per cluster.","comment":"\"FAT32   \" still at offset $52?"},
        "3420": {"synopsis":"Works out the FAT32 geometry"},
        "3434": {"synopsis":"One shift left of the four bytes at $5B73"},
        "3735": {"synopsis":"FAT16: the root directory has a fixed place and size\n1. $5B73 = the root directory's entries (offset $11).\n2. $5B65 = (the entries * 32 + 511) / 512: its sectors.\n3. $5B77 = the sectors per table (offset $16).\n4. $5B61 = the first table + the tables * the sectors per table: the root directory.\n5. $5B5D = 0: no root cluster.\n6. $5B59 = the root directory + its sectors: the first data sector."},
        "3765": {"synopsis":"One shift left of the entries: 5 passes multiply by 32"},
        "3783": {"synopsis":"Adds 511 and drops the low byte; then one shift right makes it / 512"},
        "3909": {"synopsis":"Mounted"},
        "3911": {"synopsis":"Returns with L = the result"},
        "3916": {"synopsis":"Looks for a file in the root directory by its 11-character name and fills in a file record\nfor it: the sector count read so far (0), the file's size and its first cluster\n1. The directory is read a sector at a time, 16 entries of 32 bytes per sector.\n2. Unused entries (first byte 0 or $E5), volume names and directories are passed over.\n3. FAT16 looks through the fixed root directory once; FAT32 follows its cluster chain until\n   an end mark.\nEntry: on the stack, the record's address on top, the name's address under it.\nExit: L = 1 when found, L = 0 when not.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "3944": {"comment":"No table sector cached yet"},
        "3953": {"comment":"The directory's first cluster and first sector"},
        "3995": {"comment":"FAT32?"},
        "4014": {"synopsis":"FAT32: the entries in a cluster, the sectors per cluster * 16"},
        "4025": {"synopsis":"FAT16: the entries in the root directory, its sectors * 16"},
        "4038": {"synopsis":"One shift left of the count"},
        "4047": {"synopsis":"Keeps the count at $5DA0"},
        "4057": {"synopsis":"Starts a cluster (or the FAT16 root directory): entry 0"},
        "4066": {"synopsis":"All entries looked at?"},
        "4095": {"synopsis":"The next entry: the first of a sector reads the sector"},
        "4098": {"comment":"A new sector?"},
        "4130": {"synopsis":"Reads the directory sector into the buffer; the next one goes to $5D94"},
        "4147": {"synopsis":"The next entry in the same sector: 32 bytes on"},
        "4157": {"synopsis":"Is this the entry?"},
        "4161": {"comment":"Never used?"},
        "4165": {"comment":"Deleted?"},
        "4177": {"comment":"Bits 3 and 4: a volume name or a directory?"},
        "4198": {"comment":"The name the same?"},
        "4206": {"comment":"Found: fills in the record"},
        "4233": {"comment":"The size, at offset $1C"},
        "4272": {"comment":"The first cluster's low word, at offset $1A"},
        "4287": {"comment":"FAT32?"},
        "4293": {"comment":"Its high word, at offset $14, the top 4 bits dropped"},
        "4317": {"synopsis":"FAT16: the high word is 0"},
        "4330": {"synopsis":"Stores the first cluster at the record's offset 8, and 0 at offset 0"},
        "4364": {"synopsis":"Not this entry: counts it and goes on"},
        "4386": {"synopsis":"Out of entries: the FAT16 root directory is done; FAT32 goes on to the next cluster"},
        "4400": {"comment":"The next cluster"},
        "4427": {"comment":"$0FFFFFF8 or above: the end of the chain?"},
        "4442": {"synopsis":"The cluster's first sector: the first data sector + (cluster - 2) * the sectors per cluster"},
        "4491": {"synopsis":"Not found"},
        "4493": {"synopsis":"Returns with L = the result"},
        "4498": {"synopsis":"Reads the next sector of a file found by FIND_FILE, and moves the record on\n1. The sector: the first data sector + (the cluster - 2) * the sectors per cluster + the\n   record's count masked by $5B6F.\n2. The count goes up by one; when it reaches a new cluster, the record takes the next\n   cluster from the table. The end of the file is not checked.\nEntry: on the stack, the record's address on top, the buffer's address under it.\nExit: L = 1 when read, L = 0 when not.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, BC', DE', HL'."},
        "4529": {"comment":"The record's cluster, at offset 8"},
        "4615": {"comment":"The count's place within the cluster"},
        "4668": {"comment":"Read?"},
        "4673": {"synopsis":"Counts the sector"},
        "4693": {"synopsis":"Keeps the new count; is it the first sector of a cluster?"},
        "4755": {"synopsis":"Read"},
        "4757": {"synopsis":"Returns with L = the result"},
        "4762": {"synopsis":"Reports a failure and stops: a title, the board and the core version, the card lines, then\none more low beep (110 Hz, 125 ms) than the error number, and an endless loop\n1. The title, centred: \"ZX SPECTRUM NEXT\", \"ISSUE n\" (the board ID + 2), and \"ANTI-BRICK\n   CORE major.minor.sub\" (NextReg $01 and $0E), each followed by a blank line.\n2. Bit 7 set: a report on both cards, one message number per nibble ($5B28's table): the\n   card tried first in bits 7-4, the other in bits 3-0, swapped when the cards are swapped\n   (NextReg $0A bit 5); \"SD0: \" and \"SD1: \" lines.\n3. Bit 7 clear: one line, \"SD0: \" or \"SD1: \" (the card in use) and the message.\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, the error number (a byte).\nExit: never returns.\nChanges: everything."},
        "4772": {"comment":"$5FA8 = the error number"},
        "4778": {"comment":"$5FA9 = NextReg $0A bit 5: the cards swapped?"},
        "4794": {"comment":"Deselects the card"},
        "4797": {"comment":"y = (height - 10.5 lines) / 2: the report in the middle"},
        "4834": {"comment":"NextReg $0F: the board ID"},
        "4881": {"comment":"NextReg $0E: the core's sub-minor version"},
        "4898": {"comment":"NextReg $01: the core's major and minor versions"},
        "4976": {"comment":"Red text from here"},
        "4981": {"comment":"Both cards?"},
        "4996": {"comment":"Swapped: the nibbles change places"},
        "5013": {"synopsis":"\"SD0: \" and the message in bits 3-0"},
        "5051": {"comment":"The other card's message: bits 7-4"},
        "5103": {"synopsis":"One card: \"SD1: \" when the cards are swapped, else \"SD0: \""},
        "5125": {"comment":"Swapped?"},
        "5136": {"synopsis":"\"0\""},
        "5139": {"synopsis":"The card line"},
        "5163": {"synopsis":"At 3.5 MHz, half a second, then the beeps, a quarter second apart"},
        "5175": {"synopsis":"All the beeps given?"},
        "5203": {"synopsis":"Loops here for good"},
        "5205": {"synopsis":"Sets whether the SD cards are swapped: NextReg $0A bit 5, with bit 2 set so the write\nchanges it\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, $20 to swap them or 0 not to (a byte).\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, L."},
        "5238": {"synopsis":"Swaps the SD cards over: flips NextReg $0A bit 5, with bit 2 set so the write changes it\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, L."},
        "5258": {"synopsis":"The boot ROM's program: either restarts the Next with its core from flash, or loads and runs\nthe update module from TBBLUE.FW on either SD card\n1. At 28 MHz: the palette and the text window, a black screen with white text.\n2. The core number ($5FB7, 1 unless the flash says otherwise): unless only DRIVE is held,\n   the flash's settings sector is read and searched for \"COREBOOT\".\n3. At 3.5 MHz, a 440 Hz beep for 300 ms; then back to 28 MHz.\n4. While only M1 is held, a wait of up to 249 steps.\n5. Both cards are woken, the other one first while the I key is held; the 1 key held swaps\n   the cards for good and marks it in the record ($5B07 = 1).\n6. The card in use must hold a file system with TBBLUE.FW; if not, the other one is tried.\n   A card without it is marked: no file system ($80), AUTOEXEC.BAS without TBBLUE.FW ($90),\n   neither ($A0); the other card's own result is added (0-2), and the error screen shows\n   both.\n7. The record at $5B06 (the card, the core, \"ZX NEXT AB MAGIC\") is written over the first\n   544 bytes of 8K page $22.\n8. Unless both the M1 and DRIVE buttons are held, the core is started (MAIN_BOOT_CORE).\n9. With both held, TBBLUE.FW's update module is loaded and run (MAIN_READ_HDR).\nEntry: none.\nExit: never returns: the core restarts, the module runs, or the error screen stops it.\nChanges: everything.","comment":"28 MHz"},
        "5265": {"comment":"Black border"},
        "5270": {"comment":"Clears the screen's pixels"},
        "5283": {"comment":"White ink on black paper"},
        "5296": {"comment":"Core 1 unless the flash says otherwise"},
        "5301": {"comment":"NextReg $10 bits 1-0 = 10: only DRIVE held?"},
        "5318": {"comment":"The flash's settings sector into $6000"},
        "5334": {"synopsis":"At 3.5 MHz, 440 Hz for 300 ms, then 300 ms more; back to 28 MHz"},
        "5363": {"synopsis":"Waits while only the M1 button is held, 249 steps at most"},
        "5366": {"comment":"Time up?"},
        "5379": {"comment":"NextReg $10 bits 1-0 = 01: M1 held, DRIVE not?"},
        "5397": {"synopsis":"Wakes both cards, in the other order while the I key is held, then lets the 1 key swap them\nfor good","comment":"No CMD12 at the first set-up"},
        "5407": {"comment":"The I key held?"},
        "5417": {"comment":"Swaps the cards while they are woken"},
        "5425": {"synopsis":"Sets up the card in use and then the other, which wakes both"},
        "5440": {"comment":"The cards as they were"},
        "5447": {"comment":"CMD12 at every set-up from now on"},
        "5452": {"comment":"The 1 key held?"},
        "5462": {"comment":"Swaps the cards, and says so in the record"},
        "5470": {"synopsis":"Sets up the other card again, and comes back to the one in use"},
        "5482": {"comment":"No error yet"},
        "5486": {"comment":"Card set up?"},
        "5493": {"comment":"Mounted?"},
        "5500": {"synopsis":"No file system on the card in use: $80"},
        "5507": {"synopsis":"Looks for TBBLUE.FW, its record at $5FAA; not there: is AUTOEXEC.BAS?"},
        "5544": {"synopsis":"Neither: $A0"},
        "5546": {"synopsis":"Keeps the card's result"},
        "5549": {"synopsis":"Is the card in use good?"},
        "5556": {"comment":"No: the other card"},
        "5565": {"comment":"Card set up?"},
        "5572": {"comment":"Mounted?"},
        "5576": {"synopsis":"The other card has no file system: the error screen with both results"},
        "5585": {"synopsis":"The other card: TBBLUE.FW? Not there: is AUTOEXEC.BAS?"},
        "5622": {"synopsis":"Neither: 2"},
        "5624": {"synopsis":"Adds the other card's result; the error screen"},
        "5637": {"synopsis":"Writes the record for the core or the update module into 8K page $22, at $C000 through\nMMU 6: its length, the card (8 times), the core (8 times) and \"ZX NEXT AB MAGIC\", repeated\nover 544 bytes","comment":"8K page $22 at $C000"},
        "5643": {"comment":"The card: the 1 key's mark + $20 when the cards are swapped"},
        "5663": {"comment":"8 copies"},
        "5674": {"comment":"The core number, 8 copies"},
        "5692": {"comment":"The record, then copies of it"},
        "5714": {"comment":"MMU 6 back to page 0"},
        "5720": {"comment":"NextReg $10 bits 1-0 = 11: both buttons held?"},
        "5738": {"comment":"Reads TBBLUE.FW's first sector"},
        "5755": {"comment":"Error 3: \"Error Reading TBBLUE.FW\""},
        "5763": {"synopsis":"TBBLUE.FW's first sector is in the buffer at $5DA8: its words at offsets 8 and 10 are the\nsectors to pass over and the sectors of the update module"},
        "5785": {"synopsis":"Reads the sectors before the module into the buffer, one by one"},
        "5803": {"comment":"Any left?"},
        "5824": {"comment":"Error 4: \"Error Seeking TBBLUE.FW\""},
        "5834": {"synopsis":"Loads the update module at $6000, a sector at a time"},
        "5837": {"synopsis":"Any sectors left?"},
        "5876": {"comment":"Error 5: \"Error Loading UPDATE Module\""},
        "5885": {"synopsis":"512 bytes on"},
        "5891": {"synopsis":"Deselects the card and runs the module, at 3.5 MHz"},
        "5901": {"synopsis":"Starts the Next's core: deselects the card and writes the core number with bit 7 (flash\nboot) to NextReg $10, so the FPGA reloads from flash. Should the program still run after the\nwait, error 6"},
        "5919": {"comment":"Error 6: \"ZX Spectrum Next Core Failed\""},
        "5928": {"synopsis":"Loads the ULA's first palette from index 0 (NextReg $43 = 0, $40 = 0): 16 ink entries and\nthen 16 paper entries, both from $5B3E, through NextReg $41. Then sets up the text window at\n$5FBC with the font at $19EA and the limits at $5B36: bright white text, attributes replaced\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "5941": {"synopsis":"The next ink entry; 16 done?"},
        "5958": {"synopsis":"The paper entries"},
        "5960": {"synopsis":"The next paper entry; 16 done?"},
        "5977": {"synopsis":"The text window"},
        "5992": {"comment":"Bright white ink on black paper"},
        "5997": {"comment":"Attributes replaced: mask 0"},
        "6003": {"synopsis":"Prints a text centred across the window at $5FBC, at its current y\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, the text's address.\nExit: HL = the x it starts at.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF'."},
        "6011": {"comment":"The room: the width less the left margin"},
        "6024": {"comment":"Less the text's width, halved"},
        "6076": {"synopsis":"Prints a text centred, then moves the window's y one and a half lines down\nEntry: on the stack, under the return address, the text's address.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY, AF'."},
        "6095": {"comment":"The line height * 3 / 2"},
        "6120": {"synopsis":"Reads 512 bytes of the flash through the SPI port: command $03 at $0F8000 on an Issue 2 or 3\nboard, at $438000 on an Issue 4\nEntry: HL = the buffer.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF, BC, HL.","comment":"Selects the flash"},
        "6124": {"comment":"Read"},
        "6128": {"comment":"NextReg $0F: the board ID; 0 or 1?"},
        "6146": {"synopsis":"The address, high byte first, then 512 bytes into the buffer"},
        "6161": {"comment":"A first read starts the transfer"},
        "6169": {"comment":"Deselects the flash"},
        "6174": {"synopsis":"Not reached in this ROM. When the text at DE is \"ESSENTIAL\", applies the list after it to\nthe NextRegs: a byte count, then for each register its number, an AND mask and an OR value"},
        "6191": {"synopsis":"The next register; the end?"},
        "6213": {"synopsis":"\"ESSENTIAL\": the name APPLY_ESSENTIAL compares with"},
        "6223": {"synopsis":"Looks for \"COREBOOT\" with its zero byte in a 512-byte sector; when the next two bytes are 1\nand 0, the byte after them is the core number\nEntry: HL = the sector.\nExit: L = the core number, 1 when none is found.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL, IY."},
        "6256": {"synopsis":"Not found: core 1"},
        "6259": {"synopsis":"\"COREBOOT\": the name FIND_COREBOOT looks for in the flash's settings sector, with its zero\nbyte"},
        "6268": {"synopsis":"Selects card 0 (which NextReg $0A can swap with card 1), waits, and sends $FF\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF."},
        "6274": {"synopsis":"255 passes"},
        "6282": {"synopsis":"Deselects the cards, waits, and sends $FF\nEntry: none.\nExit: none.\nChanges: AF."},
        "6288": {"synopsis":"255 passes"},
        "6296": {"synopsis":"Sets up the SD card in SPI mode\n1. 160 clocks with the card deselected, then the card selected; CMD12 and nine reads only\n   when $5FD7 is set.\n2. CMD0 until the card answers, at most 16 times.\n3. CMD8 with $1AA: rejected, the card is set up with CMD1; accepted, with ACMD41 asking for\n   high capacity. Either is repeated until the card is ready, at most 30720 times.\n4. CMD58 reads the card's OCR: its CCS bit says whether sectors are addressed by number\n   ($5FD6 = $40) or by byte; a byte-addressed card gets CMD16, 512-byte blocks.\n5. The card is deselected.\nEntry: none.\nExit: L = 3 for a card addressed by sector, 2 for one addressed by byte, 0 for no card.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "6301": {"synopsis":"Twenty bytes of $FF: 160 clocks"},
        "6310": {"comment":"CMD12 wanted?"},
        "6323": {"synopsis":"Reads nine bytes"},
        "6328": {"synopsis":"CMD0, at most 16 times"},
        "6330": {"synopsis":"CMD0; answered?"},
        "6350": {"synopsis":"CMD8 with $1AA; rejected?"},
        "6366": {"synopsis":"Up to 120 * 256 tries of the start-up command at HL"},
        "6369": {"synopsis":"Card ready?"},
        "6382": {"synopsis":"No card: deselects it"},
        "6387": {"synopsis":"Jumps to HL: CALL $18F3 runs the start-up command chosen"},
        "6388": {"synopsis":"CMD58: the OCR's first byte in B"},
        "6399": {"comment":"The CCS bit"},
        "6422": {"synopsis":"CMD16 with 512: the block length of a byte-addressed card\nEntry: none.\nExit: carry set when the card refused.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "6433": {"synopsis":"Reads one 512-byte sector with CMD17, then deselects the card\n1. A byte-addressed card gets the sector number * 512.\n2. The card's data token ($FE) is waited for; the two CRC bytes after the data are dropped.\nEntry: on the stack, the sector number's low word on top, then its high word, then the\nbuffer's address.\nExit: L = 1 when read, L = 0 when not.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "6440": {"comment":"Addressed by byte?"},
        "6447": {"comment":"CMD17"},
        "6454": {"synopsis":"Not read"},
        "6458": {"synopsis":"The data token, then 512 bytes into the buffer"},
        "6471": {"comment":"The CRC, dropped"},
        "6478": {"synopsis":"Deselects the card"},
        "6481": {"synopsis":"Turns a sector number into a byte address: times 512\nEntry: BC = the sector number's high word; DE = its low word.\nExit: BCDE = the byte address.\nChanges: F, BC, DE."},
        "6493": {"synopsis":"CMD1: the start-up command of a card that rejected CMD8\nEntry: none.\nExit: carry clear when the card is ready.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "6495": {"synopsis":"Sends the command in A with an argument of 0\nEntry: A = the command byte.\nExit: A = the card's answer; carry set unless it is 0.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "6500": {"synopsis":"Sends the command in A with the argument in BCDE\nEntry: A = the command byte; BCDE = the argument.\nExit: A = the card's answer; carry set unless it is 0.\nChanges: AF, B."},
        "6505": {"synopsis":"Carry set: the answer was not the one wanted"},
        "6507": {"synopsis":"ACMD41 with the high-capacity bit: CMD55, then CMD41 with $40000000\nEntry: none.\nExit: carry clear when the card is ready.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE."},
        "6521": {"synopsis":"Sends the command in A with the argument in DE (its high word 0)\nEntry: A = the command byte; DE = the argument's low word.\nExit: A = the card's answer; carry set when it has an error bit.\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "6528": {"comment":"Any bit but \"idle\"?"},
        "6534": {"synopsis":"Sends the command in A with the argument in DE, then reads the four bytes that follow the\nanswer\nEntry: A = the command byte; DE = the argument's low word.\nExit: A = the card's answer; BCDE = the four bytes; carry set when it has an error bit.\nChanges: AF, BC, DE, HL."},
        "6559": {"synopsis":"Sends a command: its byte, the argument high byte first, a CRC byte, then waits for the\ncard's answer. Only CMD0 and CMD8 need a correct CRC: $95 and $87; the rest get $FF\nEntry: A = the command byte; BCDE = the argument.\nExit: A = the card's answer, $FF when none came.\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "6579": {"comment":"CMD0?"},
        "6585": {"comment":"CMD8?"},
        "6593": {"synopsis":"Sends the CRC byte and waits for the answer"},
        "6598": {"synopsis":"Waits for the data token $FE. The loop goes back above the DJNZ, so it never gives up: the\ncount of ten and the carry at $19D4 are never reached\nEntry: none.\nExit: carry clear: the token came.\nChanges: AF, B."},
        "6600": {"synopsis":"The token?"},
        "6614": {"synopsis":"Reads the card until a byte other than $FF comes, at most 25600 times\nEntry: none.\nExit: A = the byte, $FF when none came.\nChanges: AF, BC."},
        "6617": {"synopsis":"Not $FF?"},
        "6628": {"synopsis":"Swaps the two halves of a byte with the Z80N's SWAPNIB\nEntry: L = the byte.\nExit: L = the byte with its nibbles swapped.\nChanges: A, L."},
        "6633": {"synopsis":"One unused byte between the code and the font"},
        "6634": {"synopsis":"The proportional font the text window prints with\n1. A header: the line height in pixels ($0A), the gap between characters ($01) and the last\n   code + 1 ($9D).\n2. From $19ED, three bytes per character from code 32: a 14-bit offset from the entry to the\n   character's bitmap, with a 2-bit left bearing in the top two bits; then the width in\n   pixels (bits 3-0) and the blank rows above the bitmap (bits 7-4).\nCodes 0-31 and those past the last print as \"?\""},
        "7015": {"synopsis":"The font's bitmaps: one byte per pixel row, the leftmost pixel in bit 7; a character's rows\nrun up to the next character's bitmap"},
        "7792": {"synopsis":"The texts, each ended by a zero byte\n1. $1E70 \"FAT16   \" and $1E79 \"FAT32   \": compared with a boot sector's file-system type.\n2. $1E82 \"ZX SPECTRUM NEXT\", $1E93 \"ISSUE %u\", $1E9C \"\" (an empty line),\n   $1E9D \"ANTI-BRICK CORE %u.%02u.%02u\": the error screen's title lines.\n3. $1EBA \"SD0: %s\", $1EC2 \"SD1: %s\", $1ECA \"SD%s: %s\", $1ED3 \"1\", $1ED5 \"0\": the card lines.\n4. $1ED7 \"TBBLUE  FW \" (TBBLUE.FW) and $1EE3 \"AUTOEXECBAS\" (AUTOEXEC.BAS) in a directory\n   entry's 8.3 form.\n5. $1EEF to $1F82: the seven messages ERROR_SCREEN picks by number through the table at\n   $5B28."},
        "8095": {"synopsis":"The start values of the 78 bytes at $5B00-$5B4D, which START copies there\n1. $5B00 = $07: the copy of port $FE (border 7).\n2. $5B06-$5B27, 34 bytes: the record MAIN leaves in 8K page $22 for the update module or the\n   core: its length ($22), 8 copies of the card used, 8 of the core number, then\n   \"ZX NEXT AB MAGIC\"."},
        "8118": {"synopsis":"\"ZX NEXT AB MAGIC\": the record's signature, ended by a zero byte"},
        "8135": {"synopsis":"The start values of $5B28-$5B35: the addresses of the seven messages, error 0 to 6\n0. \"Error Mounting SD Card\"\n1. \"Requires O/S on other SD\"\n2. \"Card Missing TBBLUE.FW\"\n3. \"Error Reading TBBLUE.FW\"\n4. \"Error Seeking TBBLUE.FW\"\n5. \"Error Loading UPDATE Module\"\n6. \"ZX Spectrum Next Core Failed\""},
        "8149": {"synopsis":"The start values of $5B36-$5B3D: the text window's limits TEXT_INIT copies in: x origin 0,\nwidth 0 (the whole 256), no right edge (1), y origin 0, height 192, a bottom edge (0)"},
        "8157": {"synopsis":"The start values of $5B3E-$5B4D: the 16 palette bytes SET_PALETTE writes to the ULA's ink\nand then its paper entries: the eight colours and their bright forms, as RRRGGGBB"},
        "8173": {"synopsis":"Unused: $FF to the end of the 8K"}
      }
    }
  },
